TASKFLOW_SKILL = """\
---
name: taskflow
description: Resume, record and run multi-session tasks (plan, validate, work, judge) with ai-governance taskflow.
---
# Task flow

- `ai-governance taskflow here` — task bound to this repository/branch; `status <id>` says what is missing and the next command.
- `taskflow new <id> --title "..."` (Jira key or slug), `bind <id> --repo <worktree>`.
- `taskflow step <id> add|done "<text>"`, `fact <id> "<text>"`, `decide <id> "<text>" --why "..."`.
- Multi-agent flow: `round <id> run --role planner|validator`, `tdd <id> red|green`, `judge <id>`, `phase <id> <next>`.
- Human gates: ask in the chat, then `approve <id> --action <a> --target <t> --user-reply "<their words>"`.
- `taskflow --help` has the whole flow. Record only what a future session needs.
"""

TEST_AUDIT_SKILL = """\
---
name: test-audit
description: Gate new tests and prune redundant ones. Use when writing tests or when asked to audit or shrink a test suite.
---
Writing a test: answer first, or don't write it.
1. What observable behavior or contract does it protect?
2. What credible regression makes it fail?
3. Why doesn't existing coverage already catch it?
Never add production seams used only by tests.

Auditing (read-only until the ledger is done): mark each test by its assertions, not its name:
R retain (contract + bug it catches) · F fix a vacuous assert · C consolidate (name the absorbing test) · D delete (name the proof that remains).
Keep one test per contract, at the cheapest real boundary. Slow or static is not a reason to delete.
Before deleting, break the production code on purpose and confirm the keeper fails; restore it.
Prefer net-negative LOC. `ws design` flags the mechanical junk (no asserts, mock-only, duplicates, sleeps).
"""

SKILLS = {"taskflow": TASKFLOW_SKILL, "test-audit": TEST_AUDIT_SKILL}


CODEX_LANGUAGE_INDEX_HEADER = (
    "# Language rules\n\n"
    "Before creating or editing a file, read the rules whose patterns match it.\n\n"
)


def codex_rules_pointer(rules_dir: Path) -> str:
    """Codex has no per-file rules, so its instructions point to the installed indexes."""
    return (
        f"- Installed rules live in `{rules_dir}`: once per session read every file in "
        "`company/` (if present); before editing code, open `language-rules.md` and read the "
        "rules whose patterns match the file.\n"
    )
